.TH STASH 1 "September 2026" "stash" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
stash \- turn Linux system telemetry into sound
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stash
.I SOURCE
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B stash \-l
.RI [ PREFIX ]
.br
.B stash \-i
.I NAME
.br
.B stash \-p
.I PRIMITIVE
.br
.B stash
.RB ( \-h | \-\-help )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stash
(Sound Telemetry Auto SHell) reads live Linux hardware and operating-system
telemetry.  With no sound-producing option it writes numeric samples to
standard output.  Sound options turn the source into a control signal for a
composable synth graph, note events, rhythm, and an ordered effects chain.
Csound provides audio-device and raw-PCM synthesis.
.PP
.I SOURCE
is a canonical source name such as
.BR cpu.usage ,
.BR cpu.cores.usage ,
.BR ram.used ,
or
.BR net.enp4s0.rx .
Use
.B \-
as the source to read numeric samples from standard input.
.SH COMMAND FORMS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a concise command reference to standard output and exit successfully.
The help flag must be the only argument.
.TP
.BR \-l " [" PREFIX "]"
List known sources or synths, optionally restricted by a case-sensitive name
prefix.  Use the prefix
.B syn
to list synths.  Unavailable hardware-specific sources are identified as such.
.TP
.BI \-i " NAME"
Inspect a source, or a synth such as
.BR syn.fm .
.TP
.BI \-p " PRIMITIVE"
Resolve and inspect a note, note list, scale, mode, rhythm, or synth declaration.
.SH OPTIONS
Options in this section follow
.IR SOURCE .
.TP
.BI \-s " TYPE[:ID][,PARAM=VALUE]..."
Add a synth node.  Canonical types are sub, fm, pm, am, ring, add, wavetable,
karplus, modal, and granular.  This option may be repeated; audible outputs
are summed before filters and effects.  Omitted IDs are assigned from the type,
for example sub, sub2, and so on.
.TP
.BI \-w " WAVE"
Select
.BR sine ,
.BR square ,
.BR saw ,
.BR tri ,
or
.BR noise .
The default is
.BR sine .
Following a synth declaration, this option sets that node's primary waveform
and may be repeated after subsequent synth declarations.  Synths without a
primary waveform reject this option.
.TP
.BI \-m " [CONTROL:]TARGET=MAP"
Add a modulation.  Omitted
.I CONTROL
means the primary source.  This option may be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-\-range " [CONTROL=]MIN..MAX"
Override the normalization range of a control.  Omitted
.I CONTROL
means the primary source.  This option may be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-v " GAIN"
Set static output gain in the inclusive range 0 through 1.  The default is
0.1 for the legacy voice.  With explicit synths this controls master gain,
whose default is 1; each synth node's gain defaults to 0.1.
.TP
.BI \-t " TRIGGER"
Define a threshold trigger:
.BR above:VALUE ,
.BR below:VALUE ,
.BR rise:VALUE ,
or
.BR fall:VALUE .
Vector-source triggers are evaluated independently at each index.
.TP
.BI \-n " NOTES"
Set note material.  Accepts one scientific-pitch note, a comma-separated note
list, a scale, or a mode.  Vector index N maps to note index N.
.TP
.BI \-r " RHYTHM"
Set a rhythm in
.B rhythm:BPM:DIVISION:PATTERN
or
.B rhythm:DIVISION:PATTERN
form.  A rhythm without embedded BPM requires
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BI \-b " BPM"
Set a positive tempo, overriding an embedded rhythm tempo.  Requires
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BI \-d " TIME"
Set the event gate duration.  Durations use
.B ms
or
.B s
suffixes.  The default is 100ms.
.TP
.BI \-a " ATTACK,DECAY,SUSTAIN,RELEASE"
Set the amplitude envelope.  Attack, decay, and release are durations;
sustain is from 0 through 1.  The default is 5ms,20ms,.8,50ms.
.TP
.BI \-\-swing " AMOUNT"
Set alternating-subdivision swing from 50 through 75 percent.  The default is
50 (straight timing).  Requires
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BI \-f " FILTER"
Append
.B lp:CUTOFF[,Q]
or
.B hp:CUTOFF[,Q]
to the effects chain.  The default Q is 0.707.  This option may be repeated.
.TP
.BI \-x " EFFECT"
Append
.B delay:TIME,FEEDBACK,MIX
or
.B drive:AMOUNT
to the effects chain.  Delay feedback is 0 through 0.95; delay mix and drive
amount are 0 through 1.  This option may be repeated.
.TP
.B \-o \-
Write headerless raw PCM to standard output instead of using the default audio
device.  The format is 48,000 Hz, two-channel, stereo-interleaved,
little-endian float32.
.SH SYNTH GRAPH
A declaration has the form
.BR TYPE[:ID][,PARAM=VALUE]... .
Every synth has numeric
.BR freq ,
.BR gain ,
.BR pan ,
.BR gate ,
and
.B mix
parameters.  Other parameters depend on the type.  Declaration assignments
set fixed configuration or numeric base values.  Use
.B stash \-i syn.TYPE
to display configuration, parameter units, ranges, defaults, audio-rate
support, and outputs for a type.
.PP
Subtractive synthesis uses type-specific parameters cutoff, q, and pulsewidth.
FM and PM use ratio or modfreq, index, and feedback; AM uses ratio or modfreq
and depth; ring uses ratio or modfreq.  Additive synthesis uses
.BR partial.N.gain ,
.BR partial.N.ratio ,
and
.BR partial.N.detune .
Karplus uses excite, damping, feedback, and brightness.  Wavetable uses
position and scan; modal uses excite, decay, brightness, and inharmonicity;
granular uses density, size, position, pitch, jitter, and spread.
.PP
The wavetable type requires
.BR table ,
modal requires
.BR model=metal|wood|glass|bell|plate ,
and granular requires a
.B sample
path.  Additive
.B partials
defaults to 8 and accepts 1 through 128.  Oscillator types accept fixed
.B wave
and, where applicable,
.B modwave
configuration.  FM, PM, AM, and ring declarations may explicitly use either
.B ratio
or
.BR modfreq ,
but not both.
.PP
All declared synths use the envelope selected by
.BR \-a .
Unknown types or parameters, missing required configuration, duplicate IDs,
invalid references, unsupported audio-rate targets, conflicting ratio and
modfreq selection, and routing cycles are rejected before Csound starts.
.SH MAPPINGS
A map has the form:
.PP
.RS
.B MIN..MAX[/CURVE][~SMOOTH]
.RE
.PP
.I CURVE
is
.BR linear ,
.BR exp ,
or
.BR log ;
the default is linear.
.I SMOOTH
is a duration such as 150ms; the default is 0ms.
.PP
Signal targets are
.BR freq ,
.BR gain ,
.BR pan ,
and
.BR gate .
Effect targets are
.BR filter.cutoff ,
.BR filter.q ,
.BR delay.time ,
.BR delay.feedback ,
.BR delay.mix ,
and
.BR drive.amount .
When several matching effects exist, an unindexed target addresses the most
recently declared matching effect.
.PP
A control may be a telemetry source or, when a rhythm is active,
.BR rhythm.gate ,
.BR rhythm.hit ,
.BR rhythm.step ,
.BR rhythm.velocity ,
or
.BR rhythm.phase .
.PP
A control may also be an audio-rate synth output named
.BR syn.ID.out .
Synth targets are
.BR syn.PARAM ,
.BR syn.ID.PARAM ,
and their additive
.B .mod
forms.  Unqualified synth targets resolve to the most recently declared node.
The unqualified targets freq, gain, pan, and gate do the same.  Telemetry and
rhythm controls may target any numeric parameter; synth outputs may target only
parameters reported as audio-rate capable by
.BR "stash \-i syn.TYPE" .
Setting
.B mix=0
keeps a synth output available for modulation while removing it from the
audible master mix.  General routing cycles are rejected before Csound starts.
.SH VALUE GRAMMAR
Numbers are decimal and may use
.BR k ,
.BR M ,
or
.B G
for powers of 1000.  A range is
.BR MIN..MAX ,
with its minimum strictly less than its maximum.  Durations require an
.B ms
or
.B s
suffix.
.PP
Notes use
.BR LETTER[ACCIDENTAL]OCTAVE ,
for example C4, C#4, or Db4, with A4 tuned to 440 Hz.
Scale and mode primitives use:
.PP
.RS
.B scale:ROOT:NAME:LENGTH
.br
.B mode:ROOT:NAME:LENGTH
.RE
.PP
Scales are major, minor, chromatic, pentatonic-major, and pentatonic-minor.
Modes are ionian, dorian, phrygian, lydian, mixolydian, aeolian, and locrian.
.PP
Rhythm divisions are 1/1, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16, and 1/32.  A pattern contains
.B x
for a hit and
.B \-
for a rest.
.SH OPERATING MODES
In telemetry mode, scalar sources write one value per line and vector sources
write one comma-separated vector per line.  No labels are emitted.
.PP
Any sound-producing option selects audio mode.  The default device receives
audio and standard output remains empty.  With
.BR \-o " " \- ,
raw PCM is written to standard output.  Diagnostics always go to standard
error.
.SH EXAMPLES
List CPU sources, list synths, and inspect aggregate CPU usage and FM synthesis:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stash \-l cpu
stash \-l syn
stash \-i cpu.usage
stash \-i syn.fm
.fi
.RE
.PP
Map CPU usage exponentially to a smoothed sine frequency:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stash cpu.usage \-w sine \-m freq=80..2k/exp~150ms
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a two-node synth graph, keeping the FM modulator out of the master mix:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stash cpu.usage \-s fm:mod,mix=0 \-s sub:voice,wave=saw \
  \-m freq=80..220 \
  \-m syn.mod.out:syn.voice.freq.mod=\-300..300
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use newline-delimited standard input as a control:
.PP
.RS
.nf
printf '0\en.5\en1\en' | stash \- \-\-range 0..1 \-m freq=100..2k
.fi
.RE
.PP
Play vector-triggered notes from a mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
stash cpu.cores.usage \-t above:95 \-n mode:E3:phrygian:12 \-d 150ms
.fi
.RE
.SH DEFAULTS
Waveform sine; frequency 440 Hz; voice or node gain 0.1; explicit-synth master
gain 1; pan 0; mapping curve linear;
smoothing 0ms; event gate 100ms; ADSR 5ms,20ms,.8,50ms; filter Q 0.707;
swing 50; sample rate 48,000 Hz; two output channels.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success, including help and clean SIGINT or SIGTERM shutdown.
One indicates invalid syntax, unavailable input, initialization or execution
failure, or an output error.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGINT and SIGTERM stop live telemetry or audio commands cleanly.
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation and examples are available at
.UR https://github.com/zalmo/stash
.UE .
